# Design system — Jagdkarte & Kompass

This is the agent-facing description of the logandaim.at visual identity. It is a rulebook, not a
history: follow it literally when building UI. Values live in
[`frontend/src/app/globals.css`](../../frontend/src/app/globals.css) and nowhere else. This file
names tokens and states rules; it never restates hex codes. The product-level intent and the
decision log live in [`specs/product/visual-identity.md`](../../specs/product/visual-identity.md).

## The identity

**Jagdkarte & Kompass.** The app reads like a map sheet from the 3D parcours. Map paper in light,
night-map green-black in dark, one map green for the single primary action. Structure comes from
what maps use: hairlines, hatching, scale bars, a dashed way with numbered marks, and mono type for
machine values. Functional UI, no presentation UI: clean, low saturation, natural rather than
technological. The product is a tool an archer trusts outdoors — clear boundaries, natural colours,
a little rounding, and none of the glass, elevation, gradients, or tonal-surface languages of Apple,
Google, or the hyper-modern web.

## Tokens

The single source is the block in `frontend/src/app/globals.css`. Every token below is defined for
light and dark. Add a token only together with an entry here.

| Token | Meaning | Use for |
|----|----|----|
| `--background` | Map paper / night map | The app shell and page bodies |
| `--foreground` | Map ink | Body text, headings, default icon colour |
| `--surface` | A sheet lying on the map | Cards, tables, dialogs, header, sidebar |
| `--surface-muted` | Secondary sheet | Table headers, hover rows, skeleton blocks, tab strips |
| `--border` | Hairline | Borders, dividers, table rules |
| `--contour` | Contour hairline | Hatching in `kontur-band`, scale-bar ticks, quiet sheet rules |
| `--primary` | Map green: single most important action | One primary button per screen, active nav item, the way |
| `--primary-foreground` | Ink on map green | Button labels and icons drawn on `--primary` |
| `--secondary` | Neutral alternative fill | Inactive tracks, muted chips |
| `--muted-foreground` | Secondary text | Hints, captions, table headers, metadata |
| `--destructive` | Irreversible action or error | Destructive buttons, inline validation, error badges |
| `--success` | Confirmed outcome | Success badges and alerts |
| `--warning` | Attention without failure | Offen/pending badges, touch-target debug outline |
| `--ring` | Focus indicator | Every `focus-visible` outline |
| `--radius` | Corner radius | `--radius-sm` through `--radius-xl` are derived from it |
| `--font-sans` | UI typeface: **Nunito Sans** | Set by the `next/font` call in `app/layout.tsx`; never name a family in a component |
| `--font-mono` | Machine values only | Map references, base URLs, token names, file names. Never for prose or numbers in a table |

Nunito Sans is the UI face; weights 400, 500, 600 and 700 are loaded, which covers every
`font-medium` / `font-semibold` / `font-bold` in the interface — add a weight to `layout.tsx` before
using one that is not listed, or the browser will synthesise it. Geist Mono is the machine face.

Dark mode is class-based (`@custom-variant dark`), with `prefers-color-scheme` as the default. The
gallery sets `.light` and `.dark` on wrappers to show both themes side by side.

## Map language

Five elements carry the identity. Use them; do not invent a sixth.

1. **Kartenkopf (sheet header).** Every page and every standalone card headed like a map sheet: a
   bold title, a mono sheet reference (`Blatt 07/32`) when a reference is meaningful, and a quiet
   hatch band (`kontur-band-quiet`) under it. The title is text, the reference is machine data.
2. **Konturband (contour band).** The separator. `kontur-band` draws it; `Separator` renders it.
   Sections are separated by a contour band, never by empty space alone or by a shadow.
3. **Weg (the way).** A route through a sequence: a dashed connector (`weg-linie`) with numbered
   marks. `WorkflowSteps` is the way. The current position is a filled triangle marker, and it is
   always named in text too ("Schritt 2 von 3").
4. **Maßstab (scale bar).** Distances are drawn on a tick scale with the rule written out
   ("ein Strich = 10 m"), not just stated. The slider is the same band as an input.
5. **Koordinaten (machine values).** Map references, IDs, distances in data rows, and file names
   render in `--font-mono`. Prose never does.

## Rules

1. **Semantic utilities only.** Use `bg-surface`, `text-muted-foreground`, `border-border`,
   `bg-primary`, `text-destructive`, and so on. Never write a raw palette class (`bg-zinc-100`,
   `text-emerald-600`, `border-red-200`) or a hex value in a component. `check:design` fails the
   build otherwise. Map patterns come from `kontur-band`, `kontur-band-quiet`, `weg-linie`, or from
   an inline style that references only `var(--…)` tokens.
2. **Contrast is a gate.** Text on a background meets WCAG 2.2 AA (4.5:1 normal text). The gallery
   measures every documented pair; a change that drops a pair below AA is not accepted.
3. **Colour is never the only signal.** `Wertungszone`, status, and validation carry their meaning
   in text or an icon too. Badges and alerts always include the word. Map patterns (hatching,
   dots, dashes) are commentary on the text, never a replacement for it.
4. **Touch targets are at least 44 by 44 pixels** on anything an archer uses with gloves: buttons,
   nav items, checkbox and radio rows, slider thumbs. The target and the drawn control are not the
   same thing: a control keeps its neighbour's height and extends its hit area with a `before`
   overlay. Never make a control taller than its row to reach 44px.
5. **Focus is always visible.** Interactive elements use `focus-visible:outline-2` with
   `outline-ring`. Never remove an outline without an equally visible replacement.
6. **One primary action per screen.** Everything else is `secondary` or `ghost`. Destructive
   actions use `destructive` and are never the primary button.
7. **German UI copy, English code.** Labels follow the ubiquitous language (`Anlage`, `Parcours`,
   `Ziel`, `Pflock`, `Runde`, `Schuss`, `Wertung`). Do not hardcode new copy in a component beyond
   the gallery examples.
8. **Spacing scale** is the Tailwind default (4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32 px). Page bodies use `p-6`,
   cards use `p-6`, form fields stack with `space-y-1.5` inside `Field`.
9. **Variants come from `cva`.** New variants are added in the component's `cva` definition, not by
   passing arbitrary classes at call sites. Public variant names stay backwards compatible.
10. **Flat by default.** Edges come from `border-border` and `--contour`, not from shadows. Only
    overlays that must float (dialogs, dropdowns) carry a shadow. No hover elevation, gradients, or
    glass; they read as presentation UI.
11. **Rounding is functional and tight.** `--radius` is small: controls use `rounded-sm`, containers
    `rounded-md`, chips and skeleton blocks `rounded-sm`. The map language is a measuring instrument,
    not a toy: nothing is pill-shaped except where the shape is the meaning (progress track, switch
    track, radio dot). A rounded corner is never decoration.
12. **A row of controls is a flex row.** Buttons are `inline-flex`; wrap trigger groups in `Example`
    (or any `flex` with `gap-2`) instead of stacking them as siblings.
13. **Natural, low-saturation colour.** Warm map paper and green-black night; primary is map green,
    not a tech accent. Dark is never pure black and never cold blue-grey. Status colours stay
    distinguishable without glowing.

## Components

Primitives live in `frontend/src/components/ui/`. They are shadcn-style: `class-variance-authority`
for variants, Radix primitives where a behaviour is non-trivial, `cn` (`clsx` + `tailwind-merge`)
for class merging.

- Only `Button`, `Alert`, and `Badge` carry status variants. Do not invent colour variants for other
  components.
- `Card` is a map sheet: `CardHeader` carries the quiet hatch band and may show a mono sheet
  reference in its description slot. Use `Card` for every standalone block of related content.
- `Separator` draws the contour band. It is the standard section divider.
- `WorkflowSteps` draws the way: numbered marks on a dashed connector, current position named in
  text and marked with a triangle. Step order comes from the data, never from colour.
- `Select` stays a native `<select>` on purpose: the platform picker is the control archers know and
  it survives gloves and one-handed use.
- `Slider` is the distance input between two Pflöcke: a 20px knob on a 6px track, with a 44px hit
  area around the knob. Do not enlarge the visible knob to reach the touch minimum.
- `CheckpointSlider` is the same distance input when the Pflöcke are known: the track is divided
  into the coloured bands between them, like a colour-picker stop bar. **The Pflock colours are
  master data** supplied by the Parcours/Ziel data and arrive as a `checkpoints` prop; the design
  system owns no Pflock palette and must never add one to `globals.css`. The value moves freely and
  never snaps to a stake. Bands are `aria-hidden`; the legend repeats every stake with its name, and
  the thumb's `aria-valuetext` names the nearest one. With no checkpoints it renders a plain track.
- `Table` rows that drag are sortable through `@dnd-kit`. The drag activator is a dedicated handle,
  never the whole row; its hit area follows the touch rule. A handle's accessible name must be
  distinct per row and must not invent a position that does not exist (an unshot Pfeil is "nicht
  geschossener Pfeil", not "Pfeil 0"). Numbers in tables are `tabular-nums` and right-aligned;
  machine values are mono.
- `Field` owns label, hint, and error placement. Use it instead of hand-assembling label + input.
- Layout components (`app-shell`, `sidebar`, `page-chrome`) and workflow components are composed
  from the primitives. The active nav item is marked by the waymark triangle plus `aria-current`,
  never by colour alone.

## Screen composition

A screen is a map sheet. This applies to app pages. **The rules
below govern appearance only.** How a journey is structured — whether a parcours is shown as a map,
a progress bar, or a plain step list — is a product decision and is not settled by this identity.

1. The page header is a Kartenkopf: title, optional mono sheet reference, quiet hatch band under it.
2. Content blocks are `Card`s (sheets) or ruled table sections; between sections sits a
   `Separator` (contour band).
3. Distances use the scale-bar pattern: the rule written out, the value in mono.
4. Numbers in rows: right-aligned, `tabular-nums`. IDs, references, coordinates: `--font-mono`.
5. Exactly one primary action per screen, in the first action row. Everything else secondary/ghost.
6. Status always as word (+ icon or pattern), colour only as second channel.

## The gallery at `/dev`

`frontend/src/app/dev/page.dev.tsx` exists only under `next dev`: `next.config.ts` adds the
`dev.tsx` / `dev.ts` page extensions only in the development phase, so `next build` never emits the
route. `build:frontend` asserts that `out/dev` and the gallery code are absent from the export.

- The gallery renders every component, variant, size, and state in a light and a dark frame on the
  same page.
- The Tokens section reads the computed CSS variables and prints the contrast ratio for every
  documented pair, with an AA pass/fail badge.
- The touch-target toggle outlines interactive elements to reveal targets under 44px.
- The Kartographie section shows the map vocabulary as elements — contour lines, elevation profile,
  compass rose, subdivided scale bar, graticule, neatline, legend — deliberately without screens or
  flows, so the identity can be judged on its own.
- The Philosophien section keeps the earlier variant line-up for reference; it is not a live
  proposal.

Workflow for a visual change: edit `globals.css` or a component, look at `/dev`, decide, then write
the result into this rule and the spec decision log **in the same commit**.

## Keeping this rule in sync

- A new or renamed token, a changed pairing, a new component variant, or a changed visual rule must
  be reflected in this file and in `specs/product/visual-identity.md` in the same commit. There is
  no "document it later".
- `check:design` (part of `mise run check`) fails when a token exists in `globals.css` but is not
  documented here, or when a raw palette class appears under `frontend/src` outside `globals.css`.
- Prose nuance cannot be checked mechanically. The PR checklist item "design rule and spec
  updated" is the human half of the same obligation.
